#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186114917 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115354 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "461c0f55-cb85-511e-b8cb-be11f8a3c250" :knowledge-point/title "Domains of Functions With Reciprocal and Radical Components"}] :question/id #uuid "9a86e942-8852-5cce-b9be-e7a5eff46d12" :question/math-academy-id "e-12350" :question/problem "Find the domain of the vector-valued function $\\mathbf f(t) = \\left\\langle \\sqrt{t+3}, \\: t^3-t^2, \\: \\dfrac{1}{t+1}\\right\\rangle.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the domain of $\\mathbf{f}$, we need the intersection of the domains of the individual components.\n\n- The domain of the 1st component $\\sqrt{t+3}$ is $t \\in [-3,\\infty).$\n\n- The domain of the 2nd component $t^3-t^2$ is $t \\in (-\\infty,\\infty).$\n\n- The domain of the 3rd component $\\dfrac{1}{t+1}$ is $t \\in (-\\infty,-1)\\cup(-1,\\infty).$\n\nThe intersection is illustrated in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/95/9577ea515e58e4db49ee5a5ef9f923bc8b34e2dc232b904c956cc1b778141f14.png)\n\nTherefore, the domain of the vector-valued function $\\mathbf{f}(t)$ is $t \\in [-3,-1)\\cup(-1,\\infty).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186114918 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115355 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "74a1499c-080d-5008-bcc3-492ef469d3dc" :knowledge-point/title "Domains of Functions With Exponential and Logarithmic Components"}] :question/id #uuid "726fa7f3-a110-5730-a734-1019fd77de9a" :question/math-academy-id "e-12362" :question/problem "Find the domain of the vector-valued function $\\mathbf f(t),$ given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{f} (t) = \\ln{t} \\, \\mathbf{i} + \\frac{1}{t-2} \\, \\mathbf{j} + e^{2t} \\, \\mathbf{k}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To find the domain of $\\mathbf{f}$, we need the intersection of the domains of the individual components.\n\n- The domain of the 1st component $\\ln{t}$ is $t \\in (0, \\infty).$\n\n- The domain of the 2nd component $\\dfrac{1}{t-2}$ is $t \\in (-\\infty,2)\\cup(2,\\infty).$\n\n- The domain of the 3rd component $e^{2t}$ is $t \\in (-\\infty,\\infty).$\n\nThe intersection is illustrated in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/2a/2a4931e8dc80192219736fc4dbc586ad2405bbb628d75bd0e17fa9616567d793.png)\n\nTherefore, the domain of the vector-valued function $\\mathbf{f}(t)$ is $t \\in (0,2)\\cup (2,\\infty).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186114919 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115356 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "553f7b9d-c387-59b0-a0d6-9624d012e414" :knowledge-point/title "Domains of Functions With Trigonometric Components"}] :question/id #uuid "a63854e6-412b-5308-be28-c05337aa4b9b" :question/math-academy-id "e-12455" :question/problem "The domain of the vector-valued function $\\mathbf f (t) = \\tan\\left(t+\\dfrac{\\pi}{6} \\right) \\, \\mathbf i + t^3 \\,\\mathbf j + e^t \\,\\mathbf k$ is \n\n$$\nt\\in (-\\infty,\\infty), \\quad t \\neq \\bbox[5pt, border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AAAAA}}.\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "To find the domain of $\\mathbf{f}$, we need the intersection of the domains of the individual components.\n\n- The domain of the 1st component $\\tan\\left(t+\\dfrac{\\pi}{6} \\right)$ is $t \\in (-\\infty,\\infty),$ except for the values of $t$ for which\n\n$$\nt+\\dfrac{\\pi}{6} = \\dfrac{\\pi}{2} + n\\pi\n\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\nt =  \\dfrac{\\pi}{3} + n\\pi ,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer.\n\n- The domain of the 2nd component $t^3$ is $t \\in (-\\infty,\\infty).$\n\n- The domain of the 3rd component $e^{t}$ is $t \\in (-\\infty,\\infty).$\n\nTherefore, the domain of the vector-valued function $\\mathbf{f}(t)$ is \n\n$$\nt\\in (-\\infty,\\infty), \\quad t \\neq \\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\dfrac{\\pi}{3} + n\\pi},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278336 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115355 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "74a1499c-080d-5008-bcc3-492ef469d3dc" :knowledge-point/title 